---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { links } from '../data/site';

const findings = [
  {
    program: 'Spark — Bitcoin Layer-2 payment protocol',
    programUrl: 'https://hackerone.com/spark',
    platform: 'HackerOne',
    severity: 'Critical',
    tone: 'crit',
    title: 'Double-spend via cooperative-exit cancellation',
    award: '$5,000',
    status: 'Validated and patched in production',
    body: `The cooperative exit path let a user leave the Layer-2 back to the Bitcoin base layer.
      Cancelling that exit at the wrong moment left the protocol holding two competing views of the
      same funds — the exit was unwound off-chain while the value it represented remained spendable.
      Reported with a working proof of concept; the fix shipped to production.`,
    tags: ['Bitcoin L2', 'state machine', 'double-spend', 'protocol logic'],
  },
  {
    program: 'Spark — Bitcoin Layer-2 payment protocol',
    programUrl: 'https://hackerone.com/spark',
    platform: 'HackerOne',
    severity: 'High · CVSS 7.1',
    tone: 'high',
    title: 'Leaf-renewal race condition enabling a double-spend',
    award: '$750',
    status: 'Paid',
    body: `Leaf renewal and leaf transfer were each safe on their own, and unsafe when interleaved.
      Driving both concurrently against the same leaf produced a window in which the renewed leaf and
      the transferred leaf were both treated as live. A classic time-of-check/time-of-use failure,
      but in a value-bearing state machine rather than a filesystem.`,
    tags: ['race condition', 'TOCTOU', 'concurrency', 'double-spend'],
  },
  {
    program: 'XRP Ledger',
    programUrl: 'https://audits.sherlock.xyz',
    platform: 'Sherlock audit competition',
    severity: 'High',
    tone: 'high',
    title: 'MPT DEX transfer-fee bypass through integer truncation',
    award: '~$1,474',
    status: 'Validated — 1 High in a competitive audit',
    body: `Fee arithmetic on Multi-Purpose Token DEX flows ran through a fixed-point helper,
      mulRatio, that truncates rather than rounds. Sizing an order so the computed fee floors to
      zero let a trader move value across the DEX and pay nothing. Individually rounding errors;
      repeated, a free-transfer primitive.`,
    tags: ['integer truncation', 'fixed-point math', 'DEX', 'fee bypass'],
  },
];

const method = [
  {
    k: 'Read the invariant, not the diff',
    v: 'Every finding above is a broken invariant — "value is conserved", "a leaf is live in exactly one place", "the fee is never zero". Those survive refactors; grep patterns do not.',
  },
  {
    k: 'Proof of concept first',
    v: 'A report without a runnable exploit is an opinion. Each of these shipped with code that demonstrates the outcome against the real implementation.',
  },
  {
    k: 'Correct myself downward',
    v: 'On a SQL injection found in production code at work, I proved the exploit and then revised the severity down — the real residual risk was denial of service, not data exfiltration. Overstating severity costs more credibility than it buys.',
  },
  {
    k: 'Concurrency is where the money is',
    v: 'Two of the three findings only exist under interleaving. Single-threaded reasoning passes review; the protocol is not single-threaded.',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title="Security research — Arnav Kshirsagar"
  description="Validated security findings: two double-spends in a Bitcoin Layer-2 payment protocol and a High-severity fee-bypass in the XRP Ledger audit. $7,200+ in awarded bounties."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <section class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">Security research</p>
      <h1>Three validated findings. $7,200+ awarded.</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I look for logic bugs in payment protocols and distributed ledgers — the class of flaw where
        every line is correct, every test passes, and value can still be created from nothing.
        Everything below was reported through a coordinated-disclosure programme and confirmed by
        the vendor or the audit platform.
      </p>

      <div class="stats">
        <div class="stat"><b>$7,200+</b><span>total awarded</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>1</b><span>Critical, patched in production</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>2</b><span>High-severity, validated</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>2</b><span>protocols: Bitcoin Layer-2, XRP Ledger</span></div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Validated findings</h2></div>
      <div class="cards">
        {
          findings.map((f) => (
            <article class="card">
              <div class="card-head">
                <span class={`badge ${f.tone}`}>{f.severity}</span>
                <span class="badge ok">{f.award}</span>
                <span class="card-meta">{f.platform}</span>
              </div>
              <h3>{f.title}</h3>
              <p class="card-meta">
                <a href={f.programUrl}>{f.program}</a> &middot; {f.status}
              </p>
              <p>{f.body}</p>
              <div class="tags">
                {f.tags.map((t) => (
                  <span class="tag">{t}</span>
                ))}
              </div>
            </article>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="callout note" style="margin-top:20px">
        <p>
          Exploit code, affected code paths and remediation detail are deliberately omitted. Report
          contents remain the disclosing programme's to publish, and several related reports are
          still open. Happy to walk through any of this in depth under NDA or in an interview.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Defensive work</h2></div>
      <p>
        The same instinct applied inward. During my internship at Powertrace I found and fixed{' '}
        <strong>8 security defects in production code</strong>. The most serious:{' '}
        <code>POST /users/</code> carried no authorization check at all, so any authenticated
        account — including a read-only demo login handed to prospects — could create a user with{' '}
        <code>{'{"*": "admin"}'}</code> permissions and log straight into it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Others included a SQL injection in a local GUI graph endpoint, proven with an exploit test,
        and a CORS weakening that I introduced while fixing an earlier issue and then closed once I
        traced what it actually exposed. Seven were pre-existing; the eighth was mine.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-head"><h2>How I work</h2></div>
      <div class="rows">
        {
          method.map((m) => (
            <div class="row">
              <div class="row-key">{m.k}</div>
              <div class="row-val">{m.v}</div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Scope and honesty</h2></div>
      <ul class="clean">
        <li>
          Figures above are <strong>awarded</strong> amounts confirmed by the programme, not
          estimates or self-assessed severities.
        </li>
        <li>
          Submission volume is not on this page on purpose. I have filed many more reports than the
          three listed; most were duplicates, informative, or wrong. The count is not the
          achievement.
        </li>
        <li>
          All testing was performed inside the published scope of an authorised bug-bounty programme
          or a public audit competition.
        </li>
        <li>Self-taught. No security coursework, no certifications — the findings are the evidence.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <div class="section-head"><h2>Get in touch</h2></div>
      <p>
        If you are running a protocol, a payment rail, or anything where an integer rounds the wrong
        way and someone gets paid for it — <a href={links.email}>email me</a>.
      </p>
      <a class="cta" href="/">&larr; Back to the rest of the work</a>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
